[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Stubbing and Spying with cy.intercept() in Cypress"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Network control is a cornerstone of reliable end-to-end testing. In Cypress, the cy.intercept() command allows you to monitor, stub, and modify network requests and responses seamlessly."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is the Difference Between Stubbing and Spying?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Spying: Listening to network traffic without altering it. This is useful for asserting that your application makes the correct API calls with the right payloads."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stubbing: Intercepting network requests and returning a controlled, mocked response instead of hitting the actual server. This isolates your tests from backend instability and slow network speeds."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Spying on Network Requests"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To spy on a request, you use cy.intercept() without providing a fulfillment body. You then assign an alias using .as() so you can wait for the request later."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"cy.intercept('GET', '/api/users').as('getUsers');
cy.visit('/dashboard');
cy.wait('@getUsers').its('response.statusCode').should('eq', 200);"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Stubbing Network Responses"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"When stubbing, you provide a fixture or an inline object as the second argument to cy.intercept(). This forces Cypress to return your mock data instantly."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"cy.intercept('GET', '/api/users', {
  statusCode: 200,
  body: [
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob' }
  ]
}).as('getMockUsers');

cy.visit('/dashboard');
cy.wait('@getMockUsers');"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Fixtures for Cleaner Code"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"For larger datasets, it is best practice to store your mock payloads in JSON files inside the cypress/fixtures directory and load them via the fixture property."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"cy.intercept('GET', '/api/users', { fixture: 'users.json' }).as('getUsersFixture');
cy.visit('/dashboard');
cy.get('.user-card').should('have.length', 3);"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for cy.intercept()"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always define your cy.intercept() commands before calling cy.visit() to prevent race conditions where the network request fires before the interception is active."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use explicit HTTP methods (e.g., 'GET', 'POST') alongside your URL patterns to avoid unintentionally catching unrelated routes."}]
